    ($SATQ$7ac5d686$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$11102-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Two-variable data: Models and scatterplots$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">An inspector begins a day of work with a large sample of shirts that need to be checked for defects. The inspector works at a constant rate throughout the morning. What type of model is best to model the number of shirts remaining to be checked for&nbsp;defects at any given time throughout the morning? </p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">A linear model with a positive slope </p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">A linear model with a negative slope </p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">An exponential growth model</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">An exponential decay model</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. Since the work is done at a constant rate, a linear model best models the situation. The number of shirts remaining is dependent on the length of time the inspector has worked; therefore, if the relationship were graphed, time would be the variable of the horizontal axis and the number of remaining shirts would be the variable of the <span class="italic"></span>vertical axis. Since the number of shirts decreases as the time worked increases, it follows that the slope of this graph is negative.<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from incorrectly reasoning about the slope. Choices C and D are incorrect and may result from not identifying the constant rate of work as a characteristic of a linear model.</p></p>
$SATQ$, true, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"7ac5d686","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"11102-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.D.","skill_desc":"Two-variable data: Models and scatterplots","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">An inspector begins a day of work with a large sample of shirts that need to be checked for defects. The inspector works at a constant rate throughout the morning. What type of model is best to model the number of shirts remaining to be checked for&nbsp;defects at any given time throughout the morning? </p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">A linear model with a positive slope </p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">A linear model with a negative slope </p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">An exponential growth model</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">An exponential decay model</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Since the work is done at a constant rate, a linear model best models the situation. The number of shirts remaining is dependent on the length of time the inspector has worked; therefore, if the relationship were graphed, time would be the variable of the horizontal axis and the number of remaining shirts would be the variable of the <span class=\"italic\"></span>vertical axis. Since the number of shirts decreases as the time worked increases, it follows that the slope of this graph is negative.<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from incorrectly reasoning about the slope. Choices C and D are incorrect and may result from not identifying the constant rate of work as a characteristic of a linear model.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959638,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"7ac5d686","skill_cd":"Q.D.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Two-variable data: Models and scatterplots","createDate":1691007959638,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"11102-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"40dbc802-011a-4ffb-91b5-8cc28aacd569","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"11102-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">An inspector begins a day of work with a large sample of shirts that need to be checked for defects. The inspector works at a constant rate throughout the morning. What type of model is best to model the number of shirts remaining to be checked for&nbsp;defects at any given time throughout the morning? </p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">A linear model with a positive slope </p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">A linear model with a negative slope </p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">An exponential growth model</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">An exponential decay model</p>\n"}},"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Since the work is done at a constant rate, a linear model best models the situation. The number of shirts remaining is dependent on the length of time the inspector has worked; therefore, if the relationship were graphed, time would be the variable of the horizontal axis and the number of remaining shirts would be the variable of the <span class=\"italic\"></span>vertical axis. Since the number of shirts decreases as the time worked increases, it follows that the slope of this graph is negative.<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from incorrectly reasoning about the slope. Choices C and D are incorrect and may result from not identifying the constant rate of work as a characteristic of a linear model.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959638,"updateDate":1691007959638,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959638, 1691007959638),
    ($SATQ$7b1a3200$SATQ$, $SATQ$32c749f0-91ae-4721-8ecd-9eac7aa1f372$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">To study the characteristics of sea stars in a group of tide pools, researchers measured the diameter of the sea stars within the tide pools. The dot plot gives the diameter, to the nearest inch, of each of the sea stars in these tide pools.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><figure class='image'><svg version="1.0.0" width="134px" height="149px" viewBox="0 0 134 149" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="A dot plot. The number line is labeled Diameter (inches). The number line ranges from 16 to 20. Refer to long description."><g><g class="xtitle-group" transform="translate(67, 129)"><text class="xtitle-group-line-0" transform="translate(0, 0)" fill="currentColor" style="font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="text-before-edge">Diameter (inches)</text></g><g class="x-axis-group" fill="none" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(22.5, 103)"><g class="tick x-tick" opacity="1" transform="translate(0.5,0)"><line stroke="currentColor" y2="6px" y1="-6px" stroke-width="1px"></line><text fill="currentColor" y="9.5" dy="0.71em" style="font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;" x="0">16</text></g><g class="tick x-tick" opacity="1" transform="translate(22.5,0)"><line stroke="currentColor" y2="6px" y1="-6px" stroke-width="1px"></line><text fill="currentColor" y="9.5" dy="0.71em" style="font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;" x="0">17</text></g><g class="tick x-tick" opacity="1" transform="translate(44.5,0)"><line stroke="currentColor" y2="6px" y1="-6px" stroke-width="1px"></line><text fill="currentColor" y="9.5" dy="0.71em" style="font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;" x="0">18</text></g><g class="tick x-tick" opacity="1" transform="translate(66.5,0)"><line stroke="currentColor" y2="6px" y1="-6px" stroke-width="1px"></line><text fill="currentColor" y="9.5" dy="0.71em" style="font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;" x="0">19</text></g><g class="tick x-tick" opacity="1" transform="translate(88.5,0)"><line stroke="currentColor" y2="6px" y1="-6px" stroke-width="1px"></line><text fill="currentColor" y="9.5" dy="0.71em" style="font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;" x="0">20</text></g><line class="x-axis-line" x1="-12" x2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1px"></line><line class="x-axis-line" id="x-axis-marker-line" x1="-12" x2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1px"></line></g><g class="dot-group" transform="translate(23,-3)"><circle cx="0" cy="93" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="0" cy="78" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="0" cy="63" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="0" cy="48" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="0" cy="33" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="0" cy="18" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="22" cy="93" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="22" cy="78" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="22" cy="63" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="22" cy="48" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="44" cy="93" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="66" cy="93" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="66" cy="78" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="88" cy="93" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="88" cy="78" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle><circle cx="88" cy="63" r="4px" fill="currentColor"></circle></g></g></svg><div role="region" aria-label="Long description for dot plot" class="sr-only"><ul>
<li>The data for the dot plot are as follows:<br/>
<ul>
<li>16: 6 dots</li>
<li>17: 4 dots</li>
<li>18: 1 dot</li>
<li>19: 2 dots</li>
<li>20: 3 dots</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div></figure></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Based on the dot plot, how many sea stars had a diameter, to the nearest inch, of <math alttext="16"><mn>16</mn>
</math> inches?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d65a7ebb-271f-49c0-b863-0878b6234f73","content":"<p><math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"5213c12d-96f7-49ac-b635-6e114d185796","content":"<p><math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"ccf62433-b99a-44b3-a4c4-357639754ba3","content":"<p><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"7ec74a84-92c2-4010-bc1b-731cf21a8964","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5213c12d-96f7-49ac-b635-6e114d185796"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. The dot plot gives the diameter, to the nearest inch, of each sea star in a group of tide pools. The number of dots above a given diameter represents the number of sea stars with that diameter, to the nearest inch. There are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="6"><mn>6</mn></math> dots above <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="16"><mn>16</mn></math> inches. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="6"><mn>6</mn></math> sea stars had a diameter, to the nearest inch, of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="16"><mn>16</mn></math> inches.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect and may result from calculation or conceptual errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the number of sea stars with a diameter, to the nearest inch, of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="17"><mn>17</mn></math> inches, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="16"><mn>16</mn></math> inches.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the number of sea stars with a diameter, to the nearest inch, of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="18"><mn>18</mn></math> inches, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="16"><mn>16</mn></math> inches.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7b1a3200","external_id":"32c749f0-91ae-4721-8ecd-9eac7aa1f372","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"d6ab4fbb-9c79-4a81-a784-84a3c4985d69","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.C.","skill_desc":"One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">To study the characteristics of sea stars in a group of tide pools, researchers measured the diameter of the sea stars within the tide pools. The dot plot gives the diameter, to the nearest inch, of each of the sea stars in these tide pools.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><figure class='image'><svg version=\"1.0.0\" width=\"134px\" height=\"149px\" viewBox=\"0 0 134 149\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" role=\"img\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-label=\"A dot plot. The number line is labeled Diameter (inches). The number line ranges from 16 to 20. Refer to long description.\"><g><g class=\"xtitle-group\" transform=\"translate(67, 129)\"><text class=\"xtitle-group-line-0\" transform=\"translate(0, 0)\" fill=\"currentColor\" style=\"font-family: Noto Serif; font-size: 15px;\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"text-before-edge\">Diameter (inches)</text></g><g class=\"x-axis-group\" fill=\"none\" font-size=\"10\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(22.5, 103)\"><g class=\"tick x-tick\" opacity=\"1\" transform=\"transla