    ($SATQ$7d721177$SATQ$, $SATQ$933baf7b-cfc3-42f9-a9f8-f184d6f97c27$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$959d07b4-8fb8-4321-950a-def1ee92cfb5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style='text-align: left;'>The density of a certain type of wood is <math><mn>353</mn>
</math><cite>&nbsp;</cite>kilograms per cubic meter. A sample of this type of wood is in the shape of a cube and has a mass of <math><mn>345</mn>
</math> kilograms. To the nearest hundredth of a <span style='text-decoration: underline;'>meter</span>, what is the length of one edge of this sample?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"af92fef2-09e4-48a2-9b29-630b0f02af5d","content":"<p><math><mn>0.98</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9469793a-0f94-4890-93ae-ba4bd2a8b724","content":"<p><math><mn>0.99</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"73c5af3d-abf6-44b4-89a3-e0590437cb6b","content":"<p><math><mn>1.01</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"76c96ec9-93ff-4cbd-9b08-d3831607cc49","content":"<p><math><mn>1.02</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9469793a-0f94-4890-93ae-ba4bd2a8b724"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the density of a certain type of wood is <math alttext="353"><mn>353</mn>
</math> kilograms per cubic meter <math alttext="left parenthesis kg slash m cubed right parenthesis"><mfenced><msup><mtext>kg/m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></mfenced></math>, and a sample of this type of wood has a mass of <math alttext="345 kg"><mn>345</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>kg</mtext></math>. Let <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> represent the volume, in <math alttext="m Superscript 3"><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, of the sample. It follows that the relationship between the density, mass, and volume of this sample can be written<br />as <math alttext="StartFraction 353 kg Over 1 m cubed EndFraction equals StartFraction 345 kg Over x m cubed EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>353</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>kg</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>345</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>kg</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="353 equals StartFraction 345 Over x EndFraction"><mn>353</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>345</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> yields <math alttext="353 x equals 345"><mn>353</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>345</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="353"><mn>353</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="x equals StartFraction 345 Over 353 EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>345</mn><mn>353</mn></mfrac></math>. Therefore, the volume of this sample is <math alttext="StartFraction 345 Over 353 EndFraction m cubed"><mfrac><mn>345</mn><mn>353</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>. Since it&rsquo;s given that the sample of this type of wood is a cube, it follows that the length of one edge of this sample can be found using the volume formula for a cube, <math alttext="upper V equals s cubed"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, where <math alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi>
</math> represents the volume, in <math alttext="m Superscript 3"><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, and <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math> represents the length, in m, of one edge of the cube. Substituting <math alttext="StartFraction 345 Over 353 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>345</mn><mn>353</mn></mfrac></math>for <math alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi>
</math> in this formula yields <math alttext="StartFraction 345 Over 353 EndFraction equals s cubed"><mfrac><mn>345</mn><mn>353</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>. Taking the cube root of both sides of this equation yields <math alttext="RootIndex 3 StartRoot StartFraction 345 Over 353 EndFraction EndRoot equals s"><mroot><mfrac><mn>345</mn><mn>353</mn></mfrac><mn>3</mn></mroot><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="s almost equals 0 period 99"><mi>s</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>99</mn></math>. Therefore, the length of one edge of this sample to the nearest hundredth of a meter is <math alttext="0.99"><mn>0.99</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7d721177","external_id":"933baf7b-cfc3-42f9-a9f8-f184d6f97c27","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"959d07b4-8fb8-4321-950a-def1ee92cfb5","uId":"67cfc8a7-76f7-4383-b803-7aa9fe064a1d","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style='text-align: left;'>The density of a certain type of wood is <math><mn>353</mn>\n</math><cite>&nbsp;</cite>kilograms per cubic meter. A sample of this type of wood is in the shape of a cube and has a mass of <math><mn>345</mn>\n</math> kilograms. To the nearest hundredth of a <span style='text-decoration: underline;'>meter</span>, what is the length of one edge of this sample?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"af92fef2-09e4-48a2-9b29-630b0f02af5d","content":"<p><math><mn>0.98</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9469793a-0f94-4890-93ae-ba4bd2a8b724","content":"<p><math><mn>0.99</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"73c5af3d-abf6-44b4-89a3-e0590437cb6b","content":"<p><math><mn>1.01</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"76c96ec9-93ff-4cbd-9b08-d3831607cc49","content":"<p><math><mn>1.02</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["9469793a-0f94-4890-93ae-ba4bd2a8b724"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the density of a certain type of wood is <math alttext=\"353\"><mn>353</mn>\n</math> kilograms per cubic meter <math alttext=\"left parenthesis kg slash m cubed right parenthesis\"><mfenced><msup><mtext>kg/m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></mfenced></math>, and a sample of this type of wood has a mass of <math alttext=\"345 kg\"><mn>345</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>kg</mtext></math>. Let <math alttext