</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["184"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["184"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="184"><mn>184</mn></math>. It's given in the table that there are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="375"><mn>375</mn></math> objects in group A and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="690"><mn>690</mn></math> objects in group C. It&rsquo;s also given that the number of objects in group C is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="p percent sign"><mi>p</mi><mo>%</mo></math> of the number of objects in group A. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="690"><mn>690</mn></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="p percent sign"><mi>p</mi><mo>%</mo></math> of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="375"><mn>375</mn></math>, which can be represented by&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="690 equals left parenthesis StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis left parenthesis 375 right parenthesis"><mn>690</mn><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfenced><mn>375</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="690 equals 3.75 p"><mn>690</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3.75</mn><mi>p</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3.75"><mn>3.75</mn></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="184 equals p"><mn>184</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></math>. Therefore, the value of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="p"><mi>p</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="184"><mn>184</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7f84b136","external_id":"b3f57376-4374-4b2d-ab34-98d991fed767","disclos